          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would changing when I eat help?",
          "answer": "Changing when you eat can help, but it depends on what you want to improve. Which of these is your main concern?\n\n- **Evening heartburn or digestion**\n- **Blood sugar**\n- **Weight**\n- **Energy**\n- **Sleep**\n\nHere is what my sources say so far:\n\n- **Heartburn:** Meal timing is one of the most useful changes for reflux that mostly happens at night. A full stomach pushes harder against the valve at the top of it, and lying down removes gravity's help. Leaving several hours between your last meal and lying down is the change most often described. Smaller meals also help. (Source: *Eating with reflux: meal size, timing, and finding individual triggers*)\n- **Blood sugar:** Meal timing matters most for people taking medicines that work at set times of day. If that applies to you, the timing should be discussed with the clinician who prescribes them, not changed on your own. (Source: *Diet for type 2 diabetes*)\n- **Weight, energy and sleep:** My search did not return passages on how meal timing affects these, so I cannot say from my sources whether it helps.\n\nIf you tell me which goal you have in mind, I can search for more specific guidance. For any change linked to a medicine or a diagnosis, please check with a clinician or pharmacist first.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
